\paragraph{Oracle ablation confirms decomposition.}
To test whether the non-significant $\gamma$ reflects poor signal quality or genuinely weak item-level effects, we replace RRF exposure scores with ground-truth binary injection labels (AUC$=$1.0). Even with perfect knowledge of which items were injected, $\gamma$ remains non-significant: MMLU $\gamma = -0.012$ (95\% CI $[-0.145, 0.119]$, PPP$=$0.41) and ARC $\gamma = +0.118$ (95\% CI $[-0.075, 0.306]$, PPP$=$0.03). This result establishes that contamination effects are too diffuse at the item level to be captured by a single scalar, regardless of detection quality. The binding constraint is not signal precision but effect heterogeneity: injection inflates some items while disrupting others (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), and a shared $\gamma$ averages over opposing directions. This strengthens the core finding: IRT's value lies in $\theta$ estimation (separating ability from noise), not in $\gamma$-based contamination quantification.

\paragraph{Gating and negative results.}
